# Rank 1055 / CVE-2026-54906: concurrent-ruby's ReadWriteLock lets any thread
# release a write lock it never took, and lets a stray read release wedge the
# lock permanently (CWE-414, CWE-667).
# lib/concurrent-ruby/concurrent/atomic/read_write_lock.rb holds
#
#   def release_write_lock
#     return true unless running_writer?
#     c = @Counter.update { |counter| counter - RUNNING_WRITER }
#
# The guard asks whether a writer is running, not whether the caller is that
# writer, so a second thread holding a reference to the lock clears the
# RUNNING_WRITER bit while the real writer is still inside its critical section
# and a further writer then acquires and runs beside it. The read side has no
# guard at all: release_read_lock decrements @Counter unconditionally, so on a
# fresh lock the counter reaches -1, and max_readers? then reads
# -1 & MAX_READERS == MAX_READERS and answers ResourceLimitError to every later
# acquire.
#
# The fixed revision tracks the owner in an AtomicReference and makes the
# release compare against it:
#
#   unless @Writer.compare_and_set(Thread.current, nil)
#     raise IllegalOperationError, 'Cannot release a write lock which is not held by the current thread'
#   end
#
# and refuses a read release when running_readers(c) is zero. The advisory
# cites no commit, so the pool row carries COMMIT_NONE; the fixed snippet
# stands on the two behaviours the advisory describes.
#
# ATTENTION, analysis gap. Scanning the file reports nothing at either
# revision, and neither does the whole atomic directory: 19 Ruby files, 0
# findings, at both revisions. Two things are missing and only the first is a
# binding.
#
#  - Ruby has no lock vocabulary at all. code.LockAcquire and core.LockRelease
#    are wired for c, cpp, csharp, go, java, objc and swift and for no other
#    language; Mutex, Monitor and synchronize have zero occurrences across the
#    Ruby binding set. So VYQL-LIFE-001 cannot fire on Ruby whatever the code
#    does. The last two snippets are that pair, differing only in whether the
#    lock is released, and both are rejected. This one is binding work rather
#    than engine work, and it is its own rank: the Java form is
#    callee.method == "lock" and "unlock", writable from Ruby's Mutex
#    documentation alone, but it does not catch this CVE, because ReadWriteLock
#    is hand-rolled on AtomicFixnum and its methods are named
#    acquire_write_lock and release_write_lock.
#  - With that vocabulary the weakness is still not stateable. It is not an
#    unreleased lock, which is all VYQL-LIFE-001 judges; it is a release whose
#    guard tests the resource instead of the caller. Nothing is wrong with any
#    line read alone -- running_writer? is a correct predicate and the
#    decrement is a correct decrement -- so there is no node to label, and the
#    discriminator is the absence of a caller-identity comparison on the path
#    to the state change. The Ruby features a binding can read are callPath,
#    functionName, literal, paramName, field, attr, assign, selector and expr,
#    none of which express a guard's subject; stating it means a rubyReview
#    observation computed in the frontend, as the 15 wired Ruby review analyses
#    are. A binding keyed on functionName == "release_write_lock" detects this
#    project rather than the weakness, since the same fail-open release written
#    as unlock or release_exclusive walks past it.
#
# Recorded in ENGINE-GAPS.md under the items that are not mechanical: what has
# to be decided is what counts as a caller-identity guard, which is judgement
# rather than a port.

test "rank1055: the write release checks that a writer runs, not that the caller is it"
  lang ruby
  file lib/concurrent-ruby/concurrent/atomic/read_write_lock.rb
  reject VYQL-LIFE-001
  code
  ```
  class ReadWriteLock < Synchronization::Object
    def initialize
      super()
      @Counter   = AtomicFixnum.new(0)
      @ReadLock  = Synchronization::Lock.new
      @WriteLock = Synchronization::Lock.new
    end

    def with_write_lock
      raise ArgumentError.new('no block given') unless block_given?
      acquire_write_lock
      begin
        yield
      ensure
        release_write_lock
      end
    end

    def release_write_lock
      return true unless running_writer?
      c = @Counter.update { |counter| counter - RUNNING_WRITER }
      @ReadLock.broadcast
      @WriteLock.signal if waiting_writers(c) > 0
      true
    end
  end
  ```

test "rank1055: the fixed revision compares the releasing thread against the recorded owner"
  lang ruby
  file lib/concurrent-ruby/concurrent/atomic/read_write_lock.rb
  reject VYQL-LIFE-001
  code
  ```
  class ReadWriteLock < Synchronization::Object
    def initialize
      super()
      @Counter   = AtomicFixnum.new(0)
      @Writer    = AtomicReference.new(nil)
      @ReadLock  = Synchronization::Lock.new
      @WriteLock = Synchronization::Lock.new
    end

    def with_write_lock
      raise ArgumentError.new('no block given') unless block_given?
      acquire_write_lock
      begin
        yield
      ensure
        release_write_lock
      end
    end

    def release_write_lock
      unless @Writer.compare_and_set(Thread.current, nil)
        raise IllegalOperationError, 'Cannot release a write lock which is not held by the current thread'
      end

      c = @Counter.update { |counter| counter - RUNNING_WRITER }
      @ReadLock.broadcast
      @WriteLock.signal if waiting_writers(c) > 0
      true
    end
  end
  ```

test "rank1055: the read release decrements with no floor, so the counter underflows"
  lang ruby
  file lib/concurrent-ruby/concurrent/atomic/read_write_lock.rb
  reject VYQL-LIFE-001
  code
  ```
  class ReadWriteLock < Synchronization::Object
    def release_read_lock
      while true
        c = @Counter.value
        if @Counter.compare_and_set(c, c-1)
          if waiting_writer?(c) && running_readers(c) == 1
            @WriteLock.signal
          end
          break
        end
      end
      true
    end

    def max_readers?(c = @Counter.value)
      (c & MAX_READERS) == MAX_READERS
    end
  end
  ```

test "rank1055: the fixed revision refuses a read release when no read lock is held"
  lang ruby
  file lib/concurrent-ruby/concurrent/atomic/read_write_lock.rb
  reject VYQL-LIFE-001
  code
  ```
  class ReadWriteLock < Synchronization::Object
    def release_read_lock
      while true
        c = @Counter.value
        raise IllegalOperationError, 'Cannot release a read lock which is not held' if running_readers(c) == 0

        if @Counter.compare_and_set(c, c-1)
          if waiting_writer?(c) && running_readers(c) == 1
            @WriteLock.signal
          end
          break
        end
      end
      true
    end

    def max_readers?(c = @Counter.value)
      (c & MAX_READERS) == MAX_READERS
    end
  end
  ```

test "rank1055: Ruby has no lock vocabulary, so an unreleased mutex is not reported"
  lang ruby
  file app/services/counter_service.rb
  reject VYQL-LIFE-001
  code
  ```
  class CounterService
    def initialize
      @mutex = Mutex.new
      @total = 0
    end

    def bump(amount)
      @mutex.lock
      @total += amount
      @total
    end
  end
  ```

test "rank1055: the released partner of that pair is not reported either"
  lang ruby
  file app/services/counter_service.rb
  reject VYQL-LIFE-001
  code
  ```
  class CounterService
    def initialize
      @mutex = Mutex.new
      @total = 0
    end

    def bump(amount)
      @mutex.lock
      @total += amount
      @mutex.unlock
      @total
    end
  end
  ```
